First run of the QA pipeline eval, small config, 3B (2026-10-10)
=================================================================
experiments/results/qa_pipeline/small-3b-2026-10-10  (599s on the M5 Pro)
Config as amended in notes/134: mask-prompt, lr 5e-5, iters_per_fact 20,
contrast_per_fact 5, context_facts 3 / context_fraction 0.5, 8 LoRA layers.

Against the predictions
-----------------------
P1  prompt_only recall on held-out paraphrases: 1.00, 1.00, 0.98, 0.94 over
    cycles 0-3 (usage 1.00 -> 0.90). Confirmed. The prompt bridge works and
    degrades slowly as the prompt grows.
P2  (revised) "some graduation in 4 cycles". Wrong in the original sense and
    in the revised one: graduated = 0 on both seeds. Seed 1 advanced 3 facts
    to stage 1 (Cloud Atlas at cycle 1, saxophone and Pachinko at cycle 2);
    seed 0 advanced none. Graduation needs stage 3.
P4  (revised) bleed gate "passes most cycles, rolls back some". Wrong at
    scale. Per attempt, bleed on the full batch:
        seed 0: 5/10, 12/20, 8/26, 20/30      seed 1: 8/10, 0/20, 18/30, 17/38
    i.e. ~40-60% of neighbor probes leak once 10+ facts train together. The
    retry drops the culprits (3, 8, 6, 10 / 4, 0, 9, 10 facts) and the small
    remainder commits in cycles 0-1; in cycles 2-3 even the remainder leaks
    (2/14, 2/10, 10/18) and the cycle rolls back. End state: 20/20 facts at
    stage 0 (seed 0), 18/20 (seed 1); 19-21 of 20 facts carry 1-2 gate
    failures, i.e. one more and they are quarantined to the prompt forever.
P5  PPL per attempt +0.5..+8.5%, cumulative drift +2.5% / +10% after the
    committed cycles. Gate never fired; the 15% ceiling held.
P6  updates: 0.25 (sleep) vs 1.00 (prompt_only). Corrections landed in the
    prompt but the sleep condition's number is dragged by rolled-back cycles.
P8  (new in 134) first-party recall failures are contrast-style deflections:
    in the 5-fact probe "I don't know that about your boss - you've only told
    me about their coffee order" while a bare prompt answers "goulash".
    Confirmed on the probe; not re-checked here.

What this says
--------------
The fixes in notes/134 make a 5-fact cycle work end to end (gates pass, 3/5
advance). They do not make a 10-20 fact cycle work. Bleed grows with the
number of facts trained together, because every added fact is another
"attribute -> value" pair the LoRA can emit for any subject, and 5 contrast
rows per fact do not keep up. The pipeline retrains ALL non-graduated facts
each cycle, so the batch only grows until something graduates - which it
cannot, because the batch bleeds. That is the loop to break.

Options, in the order I would try them
  1. Cap the training batch (e.g. 5 facts per cycle, oldest/highest-priority
     first) so each cycle looks like the probe that works. Graduation then
     takes 3 cycles per batch of 5; at 20 facts that is 12 cycles, not 4.
  2. Make contrast rows scale with the batch: for a batch of N facts, each
     fact's contrast should include the OTHER facts' attributes about the
     wrong subject ("What is your boss's favorite sports team?" -> not the
     boss, that's your son's). Cross-fact leaks ("sister's favorite food is a
     double goulash") are exactly what the current per-fact rows never show.
  3. Reword the contrast answer so it does not teach "you've only told me
     about X" (P8), or drop first-party deflection by adding positive rows
     with 2-3 other facts in context and the right fact answered.
  4. Only then revisit size. 8B under the same recipe rolled back on bleed on
     both attempts (6/10, 2/2) in the 5-fact probe; it is not a shortcut.


Second run: option 1, max_train_facts = 5 (same day)
----------------------------------------------------
experiments/results/qa_pipeline/small-3b-cap5-2026-10-10  (354s, was 599s)
Only change: sleep.maintenance.max_train_facts 5 (ConsolidationRunner.select_batch:
highest stage first, then fewest gate failures, then oldest; the rest wait
in the prompt).

                         uncapped            capped at 5
  rollbacks (8 cycles)   3                   1
  bleed on 1st attempt   5,12,8,20 / 8,0,18,17   5,0,10,2 / 4,7,3,0  (of 10-38 / of 10)
  facts with 2 failures  19 of 40            0 of 40   (7+7 with one)
  advanced to stage 1    3 (seed 1 only)     3 (seed 1 only, all in cycle 3)
  graduated              0                   0
  cumulative PPL drift   +2.5% / +10%        +6.4% / +11.5%

The cap stops the spiral: no fact is one failure from quarantine, cycles
commit, and the batch the gate sees is the one the probes pass on. It does
not produce graduation in 4 cycles, and the graduation probes now show why
(results.json "graduation"), two failure types, roughly 3:1:

  a. deflection, the contrast reflex (P8): "I don't know that about your
     boss - you've only told me about their usual coffee order", for a fact
     that a bare prompt recalls. 15 of the 22 failed probes.
  b. wrong value from another fact: "Your father's usual coffee order is a
     flat white" (it is a matcha latte), "Your colleague's favorite sports
     team is Celtic" (Urawa Reds). Cross-fact bleed, first-party this time.

One cycle (seed 0, cycle 2) rolled back with no retry because all 5 facts
were culprits (10/10 probes leaked); the runner only retries when some fact
is not a culprit. That is correct behaviour and the right outcome for a
batch that bad.

Next lever is (3) from the list above, now with evidence: the contrast
answer template is the single largest cause of non-graduation. Candidates,
cheapest first: drop "you've only told me about ..." and answer the
contrast question with a plain, short denial that does not enumerate; add
positive rows where the probed fact IS answered while 2-3 other facts sit in
the context (already half the rows; make it all of them for the first-party
rows); and raise iters for the capped batch (100 iters over ~100 rows is one
pass; 1.25-1.5 passes recalled best in the grid without bleeding).


Third run: contrast wording + full context + 1.25 passes (same day)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
experiments/results/qa_pipeline/small-3b-cap5-v2-2026-10-10
Three changes, all three candidates from the paragraph above, made before
the run:

  1. CONTRAST_ANSWERS (src/sleep/paraphrase.py) no longer enumerate what is
     known. Was "I don't know {topic} - you've only told me about {true}" /
     "You haven't mentioned {topic}. What you told me was about {true}";
     now "I don't know {topic}." / "You haven't told me {topic}." The
     true-subject derivation is gone with it. The binding is meant to come
     from first-party rows (with context) sitting next to denials for other
     subjects, not from the denial reciting the true subject.
  2. lora.context_fraction 0.5 -> 1.0: every training row, first-party and
     contrast, carries the system prompt with 1-3 other active facts, as the
     graduation probe always does.
  3. lora.iters_per_fact 20 -> 25: a 5-fact batch is ~100 rows, so 125 iters
     is ~1.25 passes (the grid's best recall without bleed) instead of 1.

Predictions, written before the result:
  P9  failure type (a), the deflection reflex, drops from 15 of 22 failed
      probes to a minority; if it does not, the reflex is in the denial form
      itself and contrast rows need a different design (e.g. answer the
      contrast question with the TRUE fact's attribute reframed, or fewer
      contrast rows).
  P10 bleed on first attempt stays in the capped run's band (0-10 of 10,
      <= 1 rollback per seed). The risk is the v1 regression: uniform
      denials made the 8B deflect everything. Guard against that reading:
      v1 had fixed subjects and no context rows; this has both.
  P11 at least one fact reaches stage 2 in 4 cycles (needs two passes in a
      row). Graduation (stage 3) is not expected in 4 cycles of a 5-fact cap
      over 20 facts.
  P12 cumulative PPL drift stays under the +15% gate but rises above run 2
      (+6.4% / +11.5%) because of the extra 25% iterations.

Result (346s):

                          run 2 (cap 5)        run 3 (cap 5 + wording/context/iters)
  rollbacks (8 cycles)    1                    1  (seed 1 c2: 2/10 then 6/6 on retry)
  bleed on 1st attempt    5,0,10,2 / 4,7,3,0   0,0,7,0 / 0,7,2,6   (of 10)
  facts with 2 failures   0 of 40              0 of 40  (4 + 12 with one)
  facts passing grad.     3 of 22 probed       5 of 24 probed
  stage 1 / stage 2       3 / 0                3 / 1  (seed 0 "a double espresso": c0 then c1)
  graduated               0                    0
  PPL drift, final        +6.4% / +11.5%       +5.6% / +11.4%
  failed phrasings        28 deflect, 7 wrong  26 deflect, 10 wrong

Against the predictions:
  P9  FAILED. Deflection is still 72% of failed phrasings (was 80%). The
      model now says exactly the new template, "I don't know your boss's
      favorite food." / "You haven't told me ...", for first-party facts.
      The reflex is not in the "you've only told me about" clause; it is in
      the denial form itself. 10 denial rows per fact against 10 first-party
      rows, and the denial is one short pattern while the first-party answer
      needs a memorized value, so at 1.25 passes the denial is what gets
      learned and it generalizes over subjects (the discrimination the rows
      were supposed to teach).
  P10 held: bleed in band, one rollback per run, nothing near quarantine.
  P11 held: one fact reached stage 2.
  P12 half: PPL under the gate; it did not rise despite +25% iterations.

Wrong-value failures (10) are all cross-fact and first-party: "cousin's dog
is named Jonas" (Jonas is the cousin's NAME, Marble the dog), "boss drives a
red Mazda MX-5" (the user's car), "neighbor's coffee order is a matcha
latte" (the father's). The model has the attributes of the batch but binds
them to the wrong subject in both directions: deny when it should answer,
answer another subject's value when it should answer its own.

Reading: contrast rows at 1:1 with first-party rows over-teach denial. The
lever is the ratio, not the wording. Next: contrast_per_fact 5 -> 2 (4 rows
vs 10 first-party per fact), keep the randomized pool, context 1.0, iters
25, and let the bleed gate + cap + retry handle the residue it was built
for. If bleed comes back above the band, the alternative is to weight
first-party rows (prepare_training_data weighted=... already repeats by
priority) rather than add denials.


