Against the predictions:
  P13 held on the ratio: deflection fell from 72% to 38% of failed phrasings
      and 42% of probed facts passed (was 21%). Absolute count (5 of 12) is
      below 8 because half the cycles never reached the probe.
  P14 under-predicted: 4 rollbacks, not 2-3, and seed 1 is the pre-cap
      spiral again (three consecutive rollbacks, one fact at two failures,
      16 of 22 at one). Retries recovered one of five failed attempts.
  P15 wrong direction: PPL drift FELL (seed 0 committed three cycles at
      <1%). Fewer rows per fact at the same iters did not cost perplexity.
  P16 failed: no stage 2 because seed 1 never committed after cycle 0.

The two runs bracket the mechanism. Denial rows trade one failure for the
other: at 10 denials per 10 first-party rows the model learns "deny" and
deflects on its own facts; at 4 per 10 it learns "attribute -> value" and
answers with the value for every subject. Neither ratio teaches the subject
binding; the 3B picks whichever shortcut the row mix makes cheaper. The
wrong-value failures in run 4 are the same cross-fact mis-binding as run 3
("cousin's dog is named Juniper", "you drive a green Land Rover Defender").

What this means for the design: the bleed gate + cap + retry do contain
the damage (no quarantine spiral at c=5, one fact at two failures at c=2),
but containing bleed and producing graduation are pulling in opposite
directions on this model at this training strength. The next experiment
should change the signal, not the ratio again:
  - keep the 5-subject diversity (the v3 lesson) but double the first-party
    rows instead of halving the denials: weighted reps or a second answer
    variant per paraphrase, so the value is seen 20x and denials 10x; and
  - iters back toward ~1.25 passes of the larger set (iters_per_fact ~35).
If that also lands on one side of the trade-off, the honest conclusion for
the 3B is that LoRA at a PPL-safe learning rate cannot hold a subject
binding for a 5-fact batch, and the comparison to run on is the 8B with the
same recipe (config-8b-mlx.yaml), where the row mix may matter less.


Decision (2026-10-10, after run 4)
----------------------------------
Keep everything from notes/133 (probe, bleed gate, registry/rollback,
replay/audit, supersede) and everything added before and between these
runs (--mask-prompt, lr 5e-5, batch cap, context rows, contrast rows).
Rolling back to the pre-133 pipeline would restore the 100% number by
removing the test that can fail, not by restoring recall; no recall number
had ever been measured on it (notes/133, "What was NOT done").

contrast_per_fact goes back to 5. The two settings fail differently and
both leave user-facing recall intact through the prompt bridge, but only
one is recoverable: at 5 a deflected fact stays in the prompt and is
retrained next cycle; at 2 rollbacks accumulate gate failures and facts hit
quarantine, which is permanent. Run 2/3 (c=5): 7 of 8 cycles committed per
run, 0 facts near quarantine. Run 4 (c=2): 4 of 8 committed, 1 quarantined,
25 of 40 one failure away.

Open, unchanged by this decision: whether a 3B LoRA at a PPL-safe rate can
hold a subject binding at all. Next experiments, in order: first-party rows
weighted 2x with the 5 denial subjects kept (iters_per_fact ~35); then the
8B on the same recipe (config-8b-mlx.yaml). The eval needs more than 4
cycles before "0 graduated" is informative at a ~30% per-pass rate.
